task: channel-elementwise-wakeups
agent: okay-42
started: 2026-09-06
branch: feature/elementwise-wakeups

why: promoted to primary by free-cont-stack. That lane went looking
  for the per-element cost in the interpreter and found runFree at
  2.5% of wall time -- while 58.3% of thread time sat in WAITING (86%
  of it Unsafe.park) and another 16.8% in TIMED_WAITING, all park.

the trap this lane must not fall into: -prof stack samples EVERY
  thread. The producer fibre parked on a full ring and JMH's idle
  threads are in that 75% too. "Three quarters parked" is NOT yet
  "the consumer parks per element", and the last two lanes both died
  of exactly this kind of unchecked inference.

so: COUNT FIRST, on the consumer path specifically. receiveBlocking is
  cb.block(k => receiveAsync(k)), which allocates a handshake Slot per
  element even when receiveAsync fires SYNCHRONOUSLY because the
  element was already buffered. Two numbers decide which fix is real:
    - callback fires synchronously (no park)  -> the cost is the Slot
      allocation, and the fix is the mirror of sendBlocking's
      OFFER-FIRST: look in the buffer, return, allocate nothing.
    - the consumer actually parks per element -> the Slot is not the
      cost, and the fix is the entry's second proposal: wake on a
      WATERMARK rather than on every pop.
  Both are named in the entry; the count says which.

ceiling, stated up front so the result is judged honestly: the effect
  machinery is ~10 points of this lane and the rest is the handshake.
  The 6.8x that chunked reading enjoys is structural -- 59 interpreter
  steps against 12085 -- and is NOT available here.
